const S=(id,title,focus,pages,observation,intuition,mathematics,application,confusion,remember,keywords)=>({id,title,focus,pages,observation,intuition,mathematics,application,confusion,remember,keywords});

const STUDY_GUIDE_SECTIONS={
  "fixed-income":[
    S(1,"The fixed-income mental map","Course architecture","pp. 5–6","The bond market contains sovereigns, agencies, corporates, mortgages, municipals, derivatives, and ETFs. Instruments are linked, but liquidity varies enormously.","Systematic fixed income is a normalization problem: describe unlike cash flows in common rate, spread, liquidity, and risk coordinates before comparing them.","Price → yield/zero curve → spread → issuer curve → residual → risk-controlled portfolio.","Use the pipeline as a debugging map. If a trade looks wrong, ask which transformation introduced the error.","A higher yield is not automatically alpha; it may compensate for duration, default, liquidity, tax, or optionality.","Normalize first; compare second.","overview pipeline bond market"),
    S(2,"Why fixed income is not equity with coupons","Market structure","pp. 7, 76","Corporate bonds have roughly ten times as many instruments as listed equities but far fewer trades. Average bond trade size is much larger.","One issuer may have dozens of bonds. Liquidity is fragmented across coupon, maturity, seniority, covenant, and issue size, so a visible last price may be stale.","Liquidity per instrument ≈ trading volume / number of instruments; this is dramatically lower in credit than equities.","Models need evaluated prices, confidence scores, and execution probabilities—not merely last trades.","Electronic trading does not imply a continuous central order book.","Many instruments, few observations.","fixed income equities liquidity instruments"),
    S(3,"Buy side, sell side, and incentives","Market participants","p. 8","Dealers earn spread on volume and manage inventory; asset managers earn fees on AUM and compete on performance and service.","The same quote has different meaning to each side. A dealer prices inventory and client value; a manager asks whether expected return survives cost and tracking risk.","Dealer revenue ≈ bid–ask spread × volume. Asset-manager revenue ≈ fee × AUM.","A realistic simulator must model counterparty behavior, inventory, information leakage, and the client’s urgency.","The buy side is not always a price taker; it chooses dealers, timing, protocol, and trade package.","Incentives shape prices.","buy side sell side dealer asset manager"),
    S(4,"Rates risk versus credit risk","Risk decomposition","p. 11","A corporate-bond yield combines the time value of money with compensation for credit, liquidity, and instrument-specific features.","First explain the risk-free curve; then explain what remains. Otherwise a Treasury move can be mistaken for a credit signal.","Corporate yield ≈ reference rate + credit/liquidity/option spread.","Hedge the intended rate exposure before judging whether a spread trade worked.","A spread is not pure default probability; it also embeds recovery, liquidity, risk premia, and technical demand.","Rates first, residual second.","rates credit decomposition spread"),
    S(5,"The zero-coupon bond","Bond mathematics","p. 12","A zero-coupon bond pays one amount at maturity, making it the atomic fixed-income cash flow.","Price is today’s value of one future payment. A larger discount rate makes that future dollar worth less today.","P=F·e^(−yT)=F·d(0,T). Therefore y=−ln(P/F)/T.","Use discount factors as the basic curve objects; coupon bonds are sums of zero-coupon pieces.","The quoted bill convention may differ from continuously compounded yield; keep conventions explicit.","Every bond is a portfolio of zeros.","zero coupon discount factor yield"),
    S(6,"Coupon bonds as dated cash flows","Bond mathematics","p. 12","A coupon bond pays periodic coupons plus principal. Each payment occurs at a different horizon.","There is no single natural discount rate for all coupons unless one deliberately compresses the curve into a yield-to-maturity quote.","P=Σ_k CF_k·d(0,t_k). Under a single yield y, P=Σ_k CF_k·e^(−yt_k).","Store the full cash-flow schedule; price, duration, spread, and hedges all depend on exact dates and conventions.","Yield-to-maturity is an internal rate of return, not the zero rate at every maturity.","Price cash flows, not labels.","coupon cash flows yield to maturity"),
    S(7,"Why price and yield move oppositely","Price–yield relation","pp. 12–13","Bond headlines say prices rise when yields fall. The relation is monotone for an option-free bond.","A higher required return discounts every promised cash flow more heavily, so the present value falls.","∂P/∂y<0. Locally, ΔP/P≈−D·Δy.","Translate yield scenarios into P&L with duration and add convexity for larger moves.","A 100 bp move is 0.01 in decimal yield, not 100.","Yield up, price down.","price yield inverse basis point"),
    S(8,"Duration as percentage sensitivity","Interest-rate risk","pp. 12–13","Long-maturity and low-coupon bonds generally move more for the same yield change.","Duration is both a cash-flow timing measure and the first derivative of log price with respect to yield.","D=−(1/P)∂P/∂y and ΔP/P≈−DΔy. A zero-coupon bond has D≈T.","Compare rate risk using duration rather than maturity alone.","Duration of 8 does not mean eight dollars; it means roughly an 8% price move for a 1 percentage-point yield move.","Duration converts yield change into percentage P&L.","duration modified macaulay sensitivity"),
    S(9,"DV01 as hedgeable dollar risk","Interest-rate risk","pp. 12–13","Traders hedge portfolios in dollars of sensitivity, not only percentages.","DV01 answers: how many dollars do I gain or lose for a one-basis-point yield change? It lets different bonds and notionals be combined.","DV01=−10^−4·∂P/∂y≈D·P·10^−4. Portfolio DV01 is the signed sum of position DV01s.","Choose hedge notionals so long and short DV01 offset, then inspect slope and curvature risk.","Equal market value is not duration neutral.","Duration is percent risk; DV01 is dollar risk.","dv01 hedge dollar duration"),
    S(10,"Callable bonds and yield-to-worst","Embedded options","p. 14","When rates fall, an issuer may refinance and call a bond, capping the investor’s upside.","The investor is short the call option granted to the issuer and therefore demands compensation.","Yield-to-worst is the lowest yield across allowable call/maturity scenarios. OAS separates spread from modeled option value.","Compare callable and noncallable bonds using OAS and scenario analysis, not raw yield alone.","A make-whole call is usually milder than a par call; call schedules and prices matter.","The issuer calls when it is good for the issuer, not the investor.","callable yield to worst oas option"),
    S(11,"Calendars, day counts, and settlement","Market conventions","p. 15","Two bonds with identical economics can calculate accrual differently because of calendars, holidays, end-of-month rules, and day-count conventions.","Fixed-income analytics is date engineering. Small convention errors contaminate prices, yields, and P&L.","Treasuries commonly use Actual/Actual; US corporates often use 30/360. Settlement conventions determine ownership and cash timing.","Use tested libraries and validate schedules against a trusted system before researching signals.","Do not hand-code approximate dates and assume the error is immaterial.","Dates are model inputs.","calendar day count settlement actual actual 30 360"),
    S(12,"Clean price, dirty price, and accrued interest","Settlement","p. 15","Bonds are usually quoted clean but settle dirty. Accrued interest transfers coupon earned since the last payment date.","The buyer compensates the seller for accrued coupon because the buyer will receive the full next coupon.","Dirty price=clean price+accrued interest. Accrued≈coupon per period×fraction of coupon period elapsed.","Reconcile cash and P&L using dirty price while displaying market quotes using clean price.","A clean-price chart can appear to jump around coupon dates even when economic value is smooth.","Quote clean; settle dirty.","clean dirty accrued coupon settlement"),
    S(13,"The bond lifecycle and liquidity","Liquidity","pp. 16–17","New issues trade actively, then many become parked in long-term accounts until news, rebalancing, reissuance, or maturity.","Liquidity is state-dependent, not a permanent security attribute. Age, issue size, benchmark status, and ownership concentration matter.","Model liquidity with age, days since trade, TRACE frequency, dealer breadth, issue size, bid–ask, and quote depth.","Allow expected trading cost and confidence to change through the lifecycle.","A stale price is not evidence of low volatility; it may be evidence of no observation.","Silence is a liquidity signal.","bond lifecycle liquidity new issue off the run"),
    S(14,"Treasury yields as reference observations","Reference curve","p. 18","Treasury securities provide observed prices and yields at selected maturities, but coupon bonds do not directly reveal every zero rate.","The quoted curve is a set of instrument observations; the discount curve is a model-consistent object inferred from them.","Each Treasury price equals the sum of coupons and principal discounted at maturity-specific discount factors.","Use liquid, correctly selected Treasury instruments and consistent conventions.","Do not treat the 10-year coupon yield as the discount rate for every cash flow before year ten.","Observed bonds imply an unobserved curve.","treasury yield curve reference"),
    S(15,"Bootstrapping the zero curve","Curve construction","p. 19","Valuation requires discount factors at all cash-flow dates, even though only a finite set of securities trades.","Solve from short to long maturities. Earlier discount factors price known coupons, leaving the next maturity’s discount factor as the unknown.","P_n=Σ_{k<n}CF_k d_k+CF_n d_n, so d_n=(P_n−Σ_{k<n}CF_k d_k)/CF_n.","Interpolate in a stable quantity such as log discount factors or forwards and check monotonicity and repricing errors.","Bootstrapping is sequential; a bad short-end input can propagate forward.","Strip known cash flows to reveal the next zero.","bootstrap zero curve discount factors"),
    S(16,"Zero rates and forward rates","Curve interpretation","pp. 18–19","A zero rate prices one payment from today to T; a forward rate prices borrowing between two future dates.","Forwards are chosen so investing long directly equals investing short and rolling forward—an arbitrage consistency condition.","z(T)=−ln d(0,T)/T; f(t1,t2)=[z(t2)t2−z(t1)t1]/(t2−t1).","Use forwards to understand curve shape and rolldown, while remembering they are not guaranteed forecasts.","The forward curve encodes today’s prices plus risk premia, not a promise about realized future rates.","Zero averages; forward is marginal.","zero rate forward rate curve"),
    S(17,"Carry: aging with yield held fixed","Holding-period return","p. 20","A bond can earn coupon and converge toward par even when its own yield does not change.","Carry isolates the mechanical effect of receiving income and shortening time to maturity under a constant-yield scenario.","Carry≈coupon income+pull-to-par under y_bond fixed.","Report carry separately from curve rolldown and spread change.","Carry does not require an issuer or Treasury curve; rolldown does.","Carry holds the bond’s yield fixed.","carry holding period return"),
    S(18,"Pull-to-par","Holding-period return","p. 20","A premium bond drifts down toward 100 and a discount bond drifts up, all else equal, as maturity approaches.","At maturity the promised redemption is par, so price convergence offsets part of coupon income.","For unchanged yield, P(t) approaches F as T−t→0.","Decompose total carry so a high coupon on a premium bond is not mistaken for equally high economic return.","Coupon income alone overstates carry for premium bonds.","Premium pulls down; discount pulls up.","pull to par premium discount"),
    S(19,"Rolldown: moving along a fixed curve","Holding-period return","p. 21","As maturity shortens, the bond occupies a different point on an unchanged curve.","On an upward-sloping curve the shorter point may have a lower yield, creating price appreciation; an inverted curve may do the opposite.","Roll P&L≈−D·P·Δy_curve, with the curve held fixed while maturity changes.","Treat rolldown as a curve exposure and stress nonparallel curve changes.","“Rates unchanged” must specify whether one yield or the whole curve is unchanged.","Carry holds yield; roll holds curve.","rolldown curve slope aging"),
    S(20,"Default, recovery, and promised cash flows","Credit risk","p. 22","Corporate cash flows are promised, not guaranteed. Default can interrupt coupons and principal repayment.","Credit value depends on both the probability of default and what investors recover afterward.","Expected loss≈PD×LGD, where LGD=1−recovery. More precisely, discount state-contingent cash flows under a pricing measure.","Do not interpret spread using PD alone; recovery assumptions and risk premia can dominate.","Expected loss is not equal to spread because spread also pays for uncertainty, liquidity, and capital.","Default frequency and loss severity are separate.","default recovery expected loss"),
    S(21,"A toy default-pricing model","Credit intuition","pp. 23–25","The deck prices a bond under survival and default states to show why risky bonds require a higher yield.","Market price is the probability-weighted value of survival payments and recovery payments, discounted appropriately.","P=Σ_t Q(survive to t)·CF_t·d_t + Σ_t Q(default at t)·Recovery_t·d_t.","Use the toy model for intuition; production models require hazard curves, recovery conventions, and risk-neutral calibration.","Historical default probability cannot be inserted mechanically into market pricing without considering risk premia.","Risky price averages state-contingent cash flows.","toy default model hazard survival recovery"),
    S(22,"What a credit spread compensates","Credit spread","pp. 25–27","Risky bonds typically yield more than government bonds, but the gap changes across issuers, maturities, and market regimes.","Spread is a bundled price for expected loss, systematic credit risk, liquidity, optionality, taxes, and supply-demand technicals.","Credit spread≈corporate yield−matched reference yield, with the exact definition depending on the chosen curve and cash-flow method.","Explain widening and tightening by component instead of calling every move a change in default risk.","A wider spread can be cheap or correctly priced for worse risk.","Spread is compensation, not a diagnosis.","credit spread widening tightening"),
    S(23,"G-spread, I-spread, Z-spread, and OAS","Spread measures","pp. 26–27, 54","Different spread measures answer different questions and may disagree for long or irregular cash flows.","G- and I-spreads compare yields at a point; Z-spread fits all cash flows to a zero curve; OAS removes modeled option value.","P=Σ_k CF_k exp(−[z(t_k)+s_z]t_k). Solve numerically for s_z.","Use Z-spread for option-free bonds and OAS for callable bonds; document curve and compounding conventions.","The same bond can have different numerical spreads without any market move if the benchmark definition changes.","Match the spread measure to the instrument.","g spread i spread z spread oas"),
    S(24,"Security master: the foundation","Reference data","p. 28","A bond model needs issuer, coupon, maturity, currency, seniority, call schedule, day count, issue size, ratings, and identifiers.","Reference data defines the cash flows and peer group. A sophisticated model cannot repair a wrong maturity or missed call feature.","Analytics are functions of both market data and security attributes: signal_i=f(price_i, cashflows_i, group_i, options_i,…).","Version the security master, validate economic fields, and record data lineage.","Identifiers are not economics; CUSIP mapping alone does not tell you which securities are comparable.","Bad reference data creates confident nonsense.","security master reference data issuer coupon maturity"),
    S(25,"Bond symbology and data lineage","Identifiers","pp. 29–30","CUSIP, ISIN, vendor tickers, TRACE symbols, and internal IDs may represent the same instrument differently.","Symbology is a join problem with time. Reissues, 144A/Reg S pairs, corporate actions, and vendor changes can create duplicates or gaps.","Maintain effective-dated crosswalks and uniqueness tests across issuer–coupon–maturity–currency–seniority.","Reject ambiguous joins and preserve the source used for each field.","A successful database join is not proof that the matched bond is economically correct.","Resolve identity before calculating value.","cusip isin symbology mapping data quality"),
    S(26,"What moves bond prices","Feature design","pp. 31–32","Rates, issuer fundamentals, sector, rating, seniority, optionality, liquidity, supply, and broad risk appetite all move prices.","A relative-value model should separate common components from bond-specific deviations.","Spread_i=g(maturity, rating, sector, seniority, liquidity, market factors)+ε_i.","Design peer groups and factors before interpreting the residual as mispricing.","A residual can be an omitted variable rather than alpha.","Explain common risk before trading the remainder.","bond price drivers rating sector seniority"),
    S(27,"Quotes, orders, and executable information","Market data","pp. 34–37","Indicative quotes, dealer runs, client orders, axes, and executable prices have different information quality.","A price is meaningful only with side, size, timestamp, protocol, and likelihood of execution.","Represent observations as (price, side, size, time, source, confidence); decay their weight as they age.","Train models on information actually available at decision time and distinguish mid estimates from achievable fills.","An order-book-looking screen may aggregate nonfirm interest.","Price without context is not market data.","quotes orders order book executable stale"),
    S(28,"TRACE: useful but delayed and incomplete","Transaction data","pp. 38–43","TRACE reports corporate-bond trades, but prints require cleaning, side inference, correction handling, and matching to other data.","TRACE reveals where trades occurred, not necessarily the full pre-trade market or who initiated every trade.","Build filters for cancels/corrections, size caps, timestamps, interdealer/client flags, and outliers.","Use TRACE for calibration and liquidity measurement, but avoid look-ahead from publication delays.","A last TRACE print is not automatically today’s fair bid or offer.","A trade print is evidence, not a complete market.","trace transaction data corrections matching"),
    S(29,"Request for quote mechanics","Execution","pp. 45–48","In RFQ, a client asks one or more dealers for prices, waits briefly, and trades with one dealer or declines.","Adding dealers improves price discovery but can leak intent and reduce dealer willingness. Speed and hit rate matter.","Expected execution value depends on quote distribution, response probability, latency, and information leakage.","Choose protocol and dealer count as an optimization problem, not a fixed habit.","RFQ is electronic, but it is not the same as anonymous exchange trading.","Optimize the whole RFQ, not only the best quote.","rfq dealer venue sequence execution"),
    S(30,"Trading cost and execution probability","Execution modeling","p. 49","The cheapest theoretical bond may be expensive or impossible to trade at modeled size.","Expected alpha must be multiplied by the chance of execution and reduced by bid–ask, impact, delay, and information costs.","Expected net value≈p(fill)·gross edge−spread−impact−delay cost−financing.","Rank opportunities by executable net value and monitor realized implementation shortfall.","A zero fill is an economic outcome, not missing data.","Unexecutable alpha is not alpha.","trading cost fill probability impact"),
    S(31,"Architecture of a bond-trading system","System design","pp. 50–51","A production system connects instruments, market data, models, signals, inventory, risk, quoting, venues, reporting, allocation, and constraints.","Each component has its own clock and failure modes; the trading decision is only as current as the slowest critical input.","Decision state_t={reference data, market data, model, positions, limits, connectivity}; action_t must be reproducible.","Log every input and model version so a fill or missed trade can be reconstructed.","A research notebook with a good backtest is not a trading system.","The system is the strategy’s delivery mechanism.","trading system architecture model signal inventory"),
    S(32,"Filtering the investable universe","Universe construction","p. 52","Not every bond should enter every model. Calls, tiny issue size, stale data, distress, short maturity, or unavailable borrow can distort results.","Universe rules define the economic question and often drive backtest performance.","Apply point-in-time filters before fitting: currency, type, maturity, issue size, price, optionality, liquidity, and data quality.","Report how returns change when rules are relaxed and guard against survivorship bias.","Filtering after seeing performance is data snooping.","The universe is part of the model.","filter universe liquidity callable survivorship"),
    S(33,"Grouping comparable bonds","Peer groups","p. 53","Curves may be fit by issuer, industry, rating, or industry×rating, with seniority and other attributes included.","Narrow groups improve comparability but provide fewer observations; broad groups stabilize estimation but mix risks.","Use hierarchical pooling: issuer information where abundant, broader priors where sparse.","Select the group based on the risk the curve should capture, and test residual stability across group definitions.","“Same issuer” does not guarantee comparability when seniority, currency, or security structure differs.","Peer quality determines residual quality.","grouping issuer industry rating senior junior"),
    S(34,"From price to a Treasury-relative spread","Normalization","pp. 54, 57","Raw price and yield cannot compare bonds with different cash-flow timing. The Treasury zero curve supplies a common baseline.","Solve for the constant spread that makes each bond’s discounted cash flows equal its market price.","P_i=Σ_k CF_ik·exp(−[z_Tsy(t_ik)+s_i]t_ik).","Fit issuer curves to spread coordinates rather than raw prices.","A curve error flows directly into every corporate spread and residual.","Reference curve first; issuer spread second.","treasury curve spread conversion z spread"),
    S(35,"Nelson–Siegel issuer curves","Curve model","pp. 58–61","Issuer spreads across maturity are noisy but often display level, slope, and hump-shaped curvature.","Nelson–Siegel compresses the curve into interpretable components without fitting every observation exactly.","f(t)=L+Se^(−Dt)+CDt e^(−Dt); z(t)=L+(S+C)(1−e^(−Dt))/(Dt)−Ce^(−Dt); z(0)=L+S, z(∞)=L.","Constrain decay sensibly, use robust weights, and examine parameter stability through time.","Four parameters with only four bonds produces interpolation, not evidence of a reliable curve.","Level, slope, curvature, decay.","nelson siegel issuer curve level slope curvature"),
    S(36,"Residuals: rich and cheap to the curve","Relative value","pp. 62–65","Observed spreads lie above or below the fitted issuer curve.","The vertical distance is a raw relative-value signal: wider-than-fit may be cheap; tighter-than-fit may be rich.","ε_i=s_i−ŝ(t_i). Standardized residual=ε_i/estimated noise_i.","Trade only persistent residuals that survive data-quality, liquidity, optionality, and risk checks.","A positive residual may reflect a bad print, smaller issue, junior rank, or imminent event—not mispricing.","Residual is a question, not an answer.","residual rich cheap fitting error"),
    S(37,"Curve quality, weights, and overfitting","Model diagnostics","pp. 65, 67","A visually smooth curve can still be wrong if observations are sparse, stale, clustered, or structurally different.","Fit quality must be judged out of sample and economically, not only by in-sample error.","Use weighted or robust loss: minimize Σ_i w_i·ρ(s_i−ŝ_i), with weights reflecting liquidity and measurement variance.","Track repricing error, leave-one-out stability, parameter jumps, residual persistence, and trade performance.","Lower in-sample RMSE does not guarantee a better trading curve.","A good curve predicts held-out bonds.","curve fitting weights robust overfitting diagnostics"),
    S(38,"Risk model and portfolio construction","Portfolio","pp. 66, 68","Many individually attractive bonds can combine into a portfolio dominated by one rate, spread, sector, or liquidity exposure.","The optimizer decides how much of each forecast to own after recognizing common risk and cost.","max_w αᵀw−λwᵀΣw−cost(Δw), subject to Bᵀw≈targets, leverage, liquidity, turnover, and position limits.","Use DV01, spread duration, issuer, sector, rating, curve, and liquidity exposures; attribute realized P&L to them.","Dollar neutrality does not imply rate, credit, or factor neutrality.","The portfolio—not the signal—is the product.","risk model optimizer covariance constraints dv01"),
    S(39,"Bond ETFs and portfolio trading","Market innovation","pp. 69–75","Bond ETFs trade continuously while many underlying bonds trade intermittently; portfolio trading executes baskets as a package.","The ETF and basket provide price discovery and diversification, but stale underlying marks can make apparent arbitrage noisy.","ETF premium/discount=(ETF market price−NAV)/NAV; hedge and creation/redemption economics depend on executable basket prices.","Model basket cost, substitution rules, inventory, and timing before labeling a premium or discount arbitrage.","NAV is an estimate when underlying bonds have not traded recently.","ETF liquidity can exceed the visible liquidity of each bond.","bond etf nav premium discount portfolio trading"),
    S(40,"Municipals and the complete trading loop","Taxes and integration","pp. 77–85","Municipal bonds add tax status, state preference, issuer type, callability, household ownership, and fragmented liquidity.","A negative nominal spread can still be attractive after tax; the correct comparison uses tax-equivalent yield and instrument-specific risks.","Tax-equivalent yield=tax-exempt yield/(1−marginal tax rate). Full loop: data→curve→signal→risk→cost→execution→attribution.","Build tax-aware peer groups and finish every project with sensitivity to costs, delays, liquidity, and alternative curves.","Tax equivalence does not remove credit, call, and liquidity risk.","Economic comparison must be after tax and after cost.","municipal muni tax equivalent systematic loop homework"),
  ],

  "quant-equity":[
    S(1,"The US investing landscape","Industry context","pp. 2–3","Capital is managed across mutual funds, ETFs, pensions, insurers, hedge funds, private funds, and individuals, each with different constraints.","A strategy’s edge depends partly on who cannot or will not hold the same trade because of mandates, liquidity, leverage, or career risk.","Opportunity≈forecast return−risk−cost−constraint shadow prices.","State whose behavior creates the opportunity and whose capital could arbitrage it away.","A large market does not imply unlimited strategy capacity.","Every anomaly has owners and constrained counterparties.","investing landscape hedge fund constraints"),
    S(2,"Hedge funds, fees, and scarce alpha","Industry economics","p. 3","Hedge-fund AUM is large, but competition, fees, crowding, and implementation reduce returns delivered to investors.","More research resources create more tested signals—not automatically more true alpha.","Investor net return=gross strategy return−management fee−performance fee−trading/financing cost.","Evaluate strategies at the investor level and track crowding and capacity.","A sophisticated process can still deliver factor beta at active fees.","Complexity is not evidence of alpha.","hedge fund fees capacity crowding"),
    S(3,"What alpha actually means","Core definition","p. 4","A manager can outperform simply by loading on rewarded systematic factors.","Alpha is the intercept left after controlling for the benchmark model; change the model and alpha can change.","R_i,t−R_f,t=α_i+β_iᵀF_t+ε_i,t.","Report gross return, factor exposures, residual alpha, standard error, and costs together.","In industry speech an “alpha factor” may mean a predictor, not proven regression alpha.","Alpha is model-relative residual return.","alpha beta residual factor model"),
    S(4,"Beta, factor exposure, and risk premium","Definitions","pp. 4–5","Assets co-move with market, value, size, momentum, profitability, investment, and other common portfolios.","Beta measures quantity of exposure; the factor premium or lambda measures the market reward per unit of exposure.","Expected excess return≈βᵀλ. Realized return=expected component+surprise.","Do not call a large beta alpha; determine whether the factor can be obtained cheaply elsewhere.","Known exposure does not mean known future return; beta is estimated from past co-movement.","Beta is quantity; lambda is price.","beta lambda risk premium exposure"),
    S(5,"Time-series trading strategies","Strategy dimension","pp. 6–7","A time-series strategy uses an asset’s own history or common predictors to decide whether that asset should be long, short, or neutral.","The comparison is an asset against itself across time—for example whether its trend or valuation is high relative to its own history.","Signal_i,t=f(X_i,t,X_i,t−1,…); position_i,t maps that forecast to exposure.","Use expanding or rolling estimation with strictly lagged inputs.","Time-series beta estimation and a time-series trading strategy are related uses of time, but they are not the same object.","Across time: compare the asset with itself.","time series strategy momentum trend"),
    S(6,"Cross-sectional trading strategies","Strategy dimension","pp. 6–7","A cross-sectional strategy ranks many assets at the same date and goes long relatively attractive names and short unattractive names.","The signal is comparative: value, quality, momentum, or another characteristic says which stock looks better than peers now.","Rank_i,t=rank(X_i,t across i); portfolio weights often demean or neutralize the ranks.","Control sector, size, beta, liquidity, and country so the ranking expresses the intended factor.","Cross-sectional does not mean the model ignores history; characteristics are often estimated from historical data.","Across assets: compare names on the same date.","cross sectional ranking long short"),
    S(7,"Fama–MacBeth stage 1: estimate exposures","Asset pricing","pp. 5–7","Factor returns vary through time, and each asset responds differently.","Stage 1 asks how much of each factor an asset carries, using that asset’s time series.","For each i: R_i,t−R_f,t=α_i+β_iᵀF_t+ε_i,t. Estimate β̂_i over time.","Check estimation window, beta stability, missing data, and errors-in-variables.","R_i,t is the return of asset i at time t; R_f,t is the risk-free return, not an ‘f asset.’","Stage 1: time gives each asset a beta.","fama macbeth stage one beta regression"),
    S(8,"Fama–MacBeth stage 2: price exposures","Asset pricing","pp. 5–7","Having exposure does not prove that exposure earns a premium across assets.","At each date, compare many assets with different estimated betas to infer the price per unit of exposure.","For each t: R_i,t=λ_0,t+β̂_iᵀλ_t+u_i,t across i; then average λ_t through time.","Use the time variation of λ_t for inference and recognize beta estimation error.","Stage 2 is a cross-sectional regression at each date, not another regression through time.","Stage 2: the cross-section gives beta a price.","fama macbeth stage two lambda cross section"),
    S(9,"Real anomaly or accidental pattern?","Research problem","p. 9","Large databases make it easy to discover return patterns that occurred by chance.","A backtest is evidence only if the hypothesis, data, and testing process make accidental discovery unlikely.","Observed performance=true effect+sampling noise+selection bias+implementation bias.","Record the research path and keep an untouched validation period.","A plausible story written after the discovery does not turn noise into causality.","Discovery is the start of verification.","anomaly real accidental data snooping"),
    S(10,"Seven questions for every new factor","Factor due diligence","p. 10","A return spread can come from risk, mispricing, data errors, microcaps, hidden exposures, or luck.","A factor needs an economic mechanism, clean construction, statistical strength, independence, robustness, investability, and a reason to persist.","Evaluate mean, volatility, t-stat, drawdown, turnover, factor loadings, liquidity, and capacity.","Build a standard research scorecard before accepting a factor.","Statistical significance is only one of the questions.","Ask why, how, after what costs, and why it remains.","factor discovery questions due diligence"),
    S(11,"Is the anomaly exploitable?","Implementation","p. 11","The largest paper return may occur in microcaps, illiquid names, or securities that are expensive to short.","Economic significance is the net payoff available at realistic scale, not the frictionless portfolio return.","Net alpha=gross alpha−spread−impact−borrow−financing−fees−delay loss.","Re-run with tradable universes, lagged signals, value weighting, borrow constraints, and impact models.","A statistically real anomaly can still be untradeable.","True in data is not necessarily available in markets.","exploitability cost microcap borrow impact"),
    S(12,"Risk-based explanations of anomalies","Economic theory","pp. 12–13","High average return may compensate for exposure that performs badly in recessions, crises, or funding stress.","A factor can look attractive in average returns while being insurance sold against bad states.","Expected return rises with covariance to priced bad-state risks, not simply with standalone volatility.","Test macro and market-state behavior, crash exposure, and marginal utility links.","Calling a pattern ‘risk’ requires identifying the risk and why investors dislike it.","Ask when the factor loses and who suffers then.","risk explanation anomaly recession crash"),
    S(13,"Behavioral explanations of anomalies","Economic theory","pp. 12–17","Investors may underreact, overreact, anchor, extrapolate, seek confirming evidence, or trade from emotion.","A bias can create mispricing when many investors make correlated errors.","Price deviation can be viewed as belief error×capital affected, limited by arbitrage capacity.","Test the mechanism directly using news, forecast revisions, flows, or post-event drift.","A named bias is not proof; it must predict more than the return spread already observed.","Behavioral story needs behavioral evidence.","behavioral finance underreaction overreaction"),
    S(14,"Limits to arbitrage","Persistence","pp. 12–13, 17","Informed traders may recognize mispricing but face short-sale, funding, benchmark, and career constraints.","Being right eventually is not enough if convergence timing is uncertain or losses force liquidation first.","Net opportunity≈mispricing×convergence probability−holding cost−funding risk−crowding loss.","Measure borrow, financing, crowding, drawdown, and catalyst horizon.","Mispricing can widen before it closes.","Limits to arbitrage explain persistence.","limits arbitrage short sale funding crowding"),
    S(15,"Cognitive shortcuts and errors","Behavioral finance","pp. 14–15","Representativeness, availability, anchoring, confirmation bias, and overconfidence distort forecasts.","Mental shortcuts are useful but can systematically overweight vivid, recent, or confirming information.","A testable behavioral signal links a measurable information event to predictable subsequent correction.","Define the bias before examining returns and test alternative risk explanations.","Do not relabel any forecast error as behavioral bias.","Bias predicts a direction of error.","anchoring availability confirmation overconfidence"),
    S(16,"Emotional shortcuts and errors","Behavioral finance","p. 16","Fear, greed, regret avoidance, disposition effects, and social pressure affect selling, chasing, and risk-taking.","Emotions can create asymmetric demand: investors may hold losers too long and sell winners too quickly, or chase crowded narratives.","Flow- or holdings-based evidence can connect the proposed emotion to price pressure and reversal or continuation.","Study investor flows, turnover, disposition behavior, and state dependence.","Emotion is not directly observed in a price chart; use measurable proxies.","Connect psychology to an observable trade.","emotional bias disposition fear greed"),
    S(17,"Finfluencers, narratives, and crowding","Information environment","p. 18","Social-media narratives can rapidly coordinate attention and trading, especially in constrained or retail-heavy names.","Attention can move demand before fundamentals, but crowded trades can reverse violently when the narrative weakens.","Measure abnormal attention, volume, short interest, options activity, and subsequent return by horizon.","Separate a predictive attention signal from exposure to liquidity and short squeezes.","Popularity is not a valuation model.","Attention can be a catalyst and a risk.","finfluencer attention narrative crowding"),
    S(18,"The quantitative investment process","System architecture","p. 19","A production strategy includes data, forecasts, risk, optimization, execution, and attribution.","A good predictor can fail because of stale data, unintended exposures, excessive turnover, or poor fills.","Data→features→alpha forecast→risk model→optimizer→orders→execution→P&L attribution.","Assign tests and owners to every stage, not only the forecast model.","A backtest is one component of the process, not the final product.","The chain is only as strong as its weakest stage.","quant investment process pipeline"),
    S(19,"The alpha model","Forecasting","pp. 19–20","Alpha models convert characteristics or events into expected relative returns.","The output should be a calibrated forecast, not merely a rank or a significant coefficient.","α_i,t=E[R_i,t+h|information_t], often standardized and neutralized before portfolio use.","Track forecast correlation, calibration, decay by horizon, and contribution by signal.","A larger raw score does not automatically mean proportionally larger expected return.","Forecast return, then verify calibration.","alpha model forecast signal decay"),
    S(20,"Risk model, optimizer, and implementation","Portfolio construction","p. 19","Signals overlap and portfolios inherit common market, sector, style, country, and liquidity risks.","The optimizer trades expected return against covariance risk, transaction cost, and constraints.","max_w αᵀw−λwᵀΣw−cost(Δw), subject to exposure, leverage, turnover, liquidity, and position constraints.","Compare ex-ante exposures with realized attribution and investigate persistent slippage.","Optimization does not create information; it allocates and sometimes dilutes forecasts.","Forecasts choose direction; risk and cost choose size.","risk model optimizer implementation attribution"),
    S(21,"Typical equity factors","Factor vocabulary","p. 20","Common characteristics include size, value, momentum, profitability, investment, quality, low risk, sentiment, and revisions.","Each characteristic is a raw descriptor; a factor return is a diversified portfolio built to represent it.","Factor-mimicking return=return of high-exposure portfolio−return of low-exposure portfolio, with controls.","Define lag, winsorization, standardization, weighting, rebalancing, and neutralization explicitly.","Book-to-market is a characteristic; HML is the portfolio return built from it.","Characteristic is input; factor is portfolio.","size value momentum profitability investment quality"),
    S(22,"CAPM as the baseline model","Asset pricing","pp. 20–21","CAPM explains expected excess return with market beta, but leaves size, value, and other patterns unexplained.","It is the simplest benchmark: investors are paid only for nondiversifiable market exposure.","E[R_i]−R_f=β_i(E[R_m]−R_f); time-series test R_i−R_f=α_i+β_i(R_m−R_f)+ε_i.","Use CAPM as a baseline, then ask whether richer models remove apparent alpha.","CAPM failure does not automatically prove mispricing; the benchmark may omit priced risks.","Start simple, then add factors deliberately.","capm market beta alpha"),
    S(23,"Fama–French three-factor model","Asset pricing","p. 21","Small-cap and high book-to-market portfolios historically exhibited returns not captured by CAPM alone.","FF3 adds diversified size and value portfolios to market exposure.","R_i−R_f=α_i+β_M MKT+s_i SMB+h_i HML+ε_i.","Interpret a strategy’s alpha only after checking MKT, SMB, and HML loadings.","HML is high book-to-market minus low; it is not high price-to-book minus low.","Market, size, value.","fama french three factor smb hml"),
    S(24,"Factor-mimicking portfolios","Construction","pp. 20–21","A characteristic is not directly tradable as a return series; portfolios translate it into a factor.","Diversifying across many stocks reduces idiosyncratic noise so the long-short return better represents the common dimension.","F_t=Σ_i w_i,t R_i,t with weights designed to be high minus low exposure and approximately neutral to unwanted dimensions.","Check concentration, microcap influence, turnover, and neutrality of the factor itself.","A factor portfolio is an engineered test asset, not a natural law.","Build exposure; diversify noise.","factor mimicking portfolio long short"),
    S(25,"The 2×3 sort and six portfolios","Portfolio sorts","p. 21","Fama–French forms two size groups and three value groups, producing six portfolios.","To study value, compare high versus low book-to-market within a size group; to study size, compare small versus big within a value group.","SMB averages small-minus-big comparisons; HML averages high-minus-low book-to-market comparisons.","Compare horizontally or vertically according to the factor while holding the other sorting dimension approximately fixed.","The six cells are portfolios containing many stocks, not six stocks.","Change one dimension; hold the other fixed.","2x3 six cells sorting portfolio"),
    S(26,"Dividend discount model","Fundamental valuation","p. 24","A firm’s value is the present value of future cash distributions to shareholders.","Accounting variables matter because they inform future dividends, growth, and required return.","V_0=Σ_{t=1}^∞ D_t/(1+r)^t.","Use DDM as an organizing identity, not a precise forecast from one dividend estimate.","A company with no current dividend can still have value if future distributions are expected.","Value is discounted future distribution.","ddm dividend discount model value"),
    S(27,"Gordon growth model","Fundamental valuation","p. 24","A mature company with indefinitely growing dividends can be summarized by constant growth and required return.","Small changes in r−g produce large valuation changes, so the model is intuitive but sensitive.","V_0=D_1/(r−g), requiring r>g.","Stress r and g and avoid using perpetual growth above the economy’s sustainable growth.","The formula breaks economically when g≥r.","Value equals next dividend divided by the discount-growth gap.","gordon growth r g dividend"),
    S(28,"Sustainable growth from ROE and retention","Fundamental valuation","p. 25","Growth can come from improving efficiency or reinvesting earnings; mature-firm growth is closely tied to retention and ROE.","Retained earnings create growth only at the return the firm earns on equity.","g=ROE×retention=ROE×(1−payout ratio).","Distinguish high growth funded by productive reinvestment from growth that destroys value.","Retaining more earnings is not automatically good when incremental ROE is below the required return.","Growth equals return on reinvested equity times retention.","sustainable growth roe retention payout"),
    S(29,"Deriving the P/B–ROE relationship","Fundamental valuation","pp. 25–26","Price-to-book varies systematically with profitability, growth, and cost of equity.","Book value is the capital base; ROE turns it into earnings; payout turns earnings into dividends.","With Gordon assumptions, P/B=(ROE−g)/(r−g). Also r≈R_f+β·market premium under CAPM.","Compare P/B only after controlling for ROE, growth, beta, and sector.","Low P/B may reflect permanently low expected ROE rather than cheapness.","P/B is largely an opinion about future ROE.","price book roe derivation cost equity"),
    S(30,"Deep value: compare economics, not ratios","Value investing","pp. 22–23, 30","Pairs such as MS versus KKR or SU versus EPD can show very different P/B because their profitability and business economics differ.","Buying the lowest multiple without understanding ROE, growth, leverage, and risk can mean buying a value trap.","Expected return is inferred jointly from P/B, ROE, g, and r—not from P/B alone.","Build peer comparisons that normalize sector and profitability, then examine the residual valuation.","‘Cheap’ is a model conclusion, not a synonym for low ratio.","Low valuation needs a profitability explanation.","deep value pair p b roe value trap"),
    S(31,"Bank case studies and rational cross-sections","Empirical example","pp. 27–29","US bank P/B ratios align strongly with ROE, and adding beta improves explanatory power.","Markets can look rational in the cross-section even during stressful periods: investors pay for sustainable profitability and discount risk.","Example regression form: P/B=a+b·ROE−c·β+error; compare R² and residuals.","Use residuals after profitability and risk controls to identify genuinely unusual valuations.","A high R² does not prove causal efficiency, but it shows raw P/B rankings omit major information.","Explain valuation before declaring mispricing.","banks regression p b roe beta case study"),
    S(32,"Implications for deep-value investors","Value investing","p. 30","High P/B often reflects expected sustainable profitability; very low P/B can reflect damaged business economics or sector-wide concern.","Value and quality are intertwined because the market prices the persistence of ROE.","From P/B=(ROE−g)/(r−g), P/B rises with ROE and falls with required return, holding other inputs fixed.","Combine value with profitability, investment, balance-sheet risk, and sector context.","Value is not automatically safer; cheap firms can be distressed or highly cyclical.","Value without quality control is incomplete.","deep value quality profitability sector"),
    S(33,"Fama–French five-factor model","Asset pricing","pp. 31–32","FF5 adds profitability and investment to market, size, and value.","The model reflects the DDM insight that expected returns relate to price, profitability, and investment jointly.","R_i−R_f=α_i+β_M MKT+s_i SMB+h_i HML+r_i RMW+c_i CMA+ε_i.","Test whether a strategy’s alpha survives FF5 and whether exposures match the intended economic story.","RMW is robust minus weak profitability; CMA is conservative minus aggressive investment.","Market, size, value, profitability, investment.","ff5 rmw cma five factor"),
    S(34,"What zero alpha means in a factor model","Model interpretation","p. 32","If chosen factors capture all cross-sectional variation in expected returns, correctly priced test assets have zero intercepts.","The model says exposures explain average returns; residuals should be unpredictable and average to zero.","Null for each asset: α_i=0. Joint null: α_1=…=α_N=0.","Evaluate both individual alphas and their joint economic magnitude.","Estimated alpha can be nonzero from noise even when the model is correct.","A good model leaves no systematic intercept.","zero alpha factor model intercept"),
    S(35,"Reading FF5 portfolio evidence","Empirical results","p. 33","Value effects are stronger among small caps; size effects often appear through low megacap returns; high investment can associate with lower returns.","Average portfolio returns reveal interactions—one characteristic’s effect can depend on another.","Compare cell means while fixing other sort dimensions and examine monotonicity, spread, and standard error.","Do not summarize a factor with one aggregate premium if the cross-section is highly uneven.","A factor’s average return can be driven by one corner of the universe.","Read the cells, not only the label.","ff5 performance cells small cap investment"),
    S(36,"Triple sorts, four-way sorts, and 81 cells","Portfolio sorts","pp. 34–35","Characteristics are correlated, so double sorts may fail to isolate value, profitability, and investment.","More sorting dimensions improve control but fragment the universe into smaller, noisier portfolios.","Three groups on four characteristics create 3^4=81 cells; two groups on four create 2^4=16.","Choose breakpoints that preserve diversification, use NYSE thresholds where appropriate, and report cell counts.","Eighty-one means portfolio cells, not 81 individual stocks.","Isolation rises as diversification falls.","triple sort four way 81 cells breakpoints"),
    S(37,"Factor correlation and double counting","Factor diagnostics","p. 34","Value, profitability, and investment characteristics are correlated; HML and RMW can share many short positions.","Two named factors may represent overlapping economic bets, so adding both does not necessarily add independent information.","Inspect factor correlation, regression spanning, marginal Sharpe ratio, and common holdings.","Orthogonalize carefully, compare definitions, and attribute portfolio risk to shared positions.","Low correlation is not the only measure of independence; nonlinear and tail overlap matter.","Different names can hide the same trade.","factor correlation hml rmw overlap"),
    S(38,"GRS and comparing asset-pricing models","Model testing","pp. 36–39","Most factor models are rejected somewhere; model choice is comparative rather than a search for perfection.","GRS asks whether all test-portfolio alphas are jointly zero while accounting for their covariance.","H0: α_1=…=α_N=0. Lower GRS statistics indicate smaller joint pricing errors for the same test assets.","Compare CAPM, FF3, Carhart, q-factor, and FF5 on common data and economically relevant test portfolios.","A lower GRS statistic does not guarantee the model is the best forecasting or trading model.","Use joint errors to compare imperfect models.","grs model comparison capm ff3 q factor"),
    S(39,"The factor zoo, p-hacking, and error types","Research discipline","p. 40","In a replication of 447 anomalies, many became insignificant after microcap controls, value weighting, stricter t-stat thresholds, and q-factor adjustment.","If hundreds of ideas are tested, t≈2 is no longer surprising. Selection makes the published winner look stronger than an untouched test would.","t=estimate/standard error. Type I: reject a true null. Type II: fail to reject a false null. Stricter hurdles reduce Type I but raise Type II risk.","Use |t|>3 as one screen, plus out-of-sample, multiple-testing controls, economic mechanism, and net-cost evidence.","Type II means real alpha exists but the test misses it—not that you found it and deliberately rejected it.","More trials require stronger evidence.","factor zoo p hacking t statistic type one type two"),
    S(40,"Century evidence and project diagnostics","From evidence to strategy","pp. 41–50","Long histories across countries show recurring size, value, dividend-yield, momentum, and low-risk patterns, but performance varies by era and market.","Persistence across independent samples strengthens evidence, yet a project must still show how the signal becomes an executable portfolio.","Momentum commonly uses R_(t−12,t−1), skipping the most recent month. Project chain: data→signal→weights→PnL→factor/cost diagnostics.","Report gross/net returns, delay curves, spread and impact assumptions, borrow, liquidity-universe changes, capacity, subperiods, and known-factor exposure.","A century chart is not a guarantee; definitions, data quality, and implementation evolve.","Replicate, stress, explain, then trade.","century evidence momentum low risk project diagnostics"),
  ]
};
